  <section class="brx-sec brx-tint" id="cost">
    <div class="brx-wrap">
      <h2>How much does radiator valve replacement cost in Liverpool?</h2>
      <p class="brx-answer">A TRV supplied and fitted costs £65 including VAT. Up to six TRVs cost £295.</p>
      <div class="brx-table-wrap">
        <table class="brx-table stk stk-price">
          <thead><tr><th>Job</th><th>Price inc VAT</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td data-label="Job"><strong>TRV supplied and fitted</strong></td><td data-label="Price inc VAT">£65 each</td><td data-label="Notes">Matching lockshield included where needed</td></tr>
            <tr><td data-label="Job"><strong>Up to 6 TRVs supplied and fitted</strong></td><td data-label="Price inc VAT">£295</td><td data-label="Notes">One visit, one drain down</td></tr>
            <tr><td data-label="Job"><strong>Manual (wheelhead) valve pair swap</strong></td><td data-label="Price inc VAT">£65 per radiator</td><td data-label="Notes">Simple on/off valves, like for like</td></tr>
            <tr><td data-label="Job"><strong>Fit your own smart radiator valves</strong></td><td data-label="Price inc VAT">£65 per radiator</td><td data-label="Notes">Fitted, paired and tested</td></tr>
            <tr><td data-label="Job"><strong>Leaking valve repair</strong></td><td data-label="Price inc VAT">First hour £120</td><td data-label="Notes">£150 evenings and weekends, £180 bank holidays</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <h3>What is included</h3>
      <ul class="sg-ticks">
        <li>A new quality thermostatic valve, fitted to your radiator.</li>
        <li>A matching lockshield valve where one is needed.</li>
        <li>Draining down, or a freezing kit on the pipe, as the job needs.</li>
        <li>The system refilled, bled and pressure checked.</li>
        <li>A leak check on every joint before we leave.</li>
        <li>Our 12-month workmanship guarantee.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>There is no call-out fee. If something else needs fixing, we tell you the price before we do it.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="brx-sec" id="types">
    <div class="brx-wrap">
      <h2>What is the difference between a TRV, a lockshield and a manual valve?</h2>
      <p class="brx-answer">Most radiators have two valves. One controls the heat. The other, the lockshield, balances the flow and is set once.</p>
      <div class="sg-cols">
        <div class="sg-box">
          <h3>TRV (thermostatic radiator valve)</h3>
          <p>The head has numbers, often from * to 5. It senses the air in the room. When the room is warm enough, it shuts off the hot water. Turn it down in spare rooms and up in the rooms you use.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="sg-box">
          <h3>Lockshield valve</h3>
          <p>It sits on the other end, under a plain plastic cap. It is not for daily use. Engineers set it to balance the system, so every radiator heats up evenly.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="sg-box">
          <h3>Manual (wheelhead) valve</h3>
          <p>A simple on/off valve with no numbers. It does not sense the room. The radiator is either on or off. Many older Liverpool homes still have them.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p style="margin-top:1rem;">Swapping manual valves for TRVs gives you room-by-room control. It can help cut wasted heating, especially in larger terraces and semis where some rooms are rarely used.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="brx-sec brx-tint" id="bypass">
    <div class="brx-wrap">
      <h2>Why is one radiator usually left without a TRV?</h2>
      <p class="brx-answer">Water needs a path when every TRV has shut. So one radiator, often in the hall, is usually left without a TRV.</p>
      <p>Picture a warm day. Every TRV in the house closes. The pump is still running, but the water has nowhere to go. That can make the system noisy, and it can strain the boiler.</p>
      <p>Many modern systems have an automatic bypass valve, which solves this. But a radiator with no TRV is still common. It is often in the hall, where the room thermostat lives.</p>
      <div class="brx-note"><p><strong>Keep the thermostat and TRV apart.</strong> A TRV in the same room as your thermostat can fight it. The TRV shuts first, the room stays cool, and the boiler keeps firing. We will tell you which radiator to leave as it is.</p></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="brx-sec" id="stuck">
    <div class="brx-wrap">
      <h2>Why is my TRV stuck, and can I fix it?</h2>
      <p class="brx-answer">TRV pins often stick after the summer, when the heating has been off. You can often free one yourself in a few minutes.</p>
      <ol class="sg-steps">
        <li><strong>Turn the head to 5.</strong> Then unscrew the head or pop off its clip.</li>
        <li><strong>Find the pin.</strong> It is a small metal pin sticking out of the valve body.</li>
        <li><strong>Press it gently.</strong> Push it in and let it spring back a few times. Do not force it.</li>
        <li><strong>Refit the head.</strong> Wait a few minutes and feel the radiator.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>If the pin will not move, or the radiator stays cold, call us. The valve may be worn out. The radiator may also need bleeding or balancing. A new TRV is £65 fitted.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="brx-sec brx-tint" id="leaks">
    <div class="brx-wrap">
      <h2>What causes a leaking radiator valve?</h2>
      <p class="brx-answer">Most leaks come from a worn spindle seal, a loose nut or a worn valve tail. We charge our first-hour rate of £120 on weekdays, and most are fixed in that hour.</p>
      <ul class="brx-list">
        <li><strong>Drip from under the cap or head.</strong> Usually a worn gland seal around the spindle. Often a quick fix.</li>
        <li><strong>Drip from the nut on the pipe.</strong> Often a loose nut or a worn olive.</li>
        <li><strong>Drip where the valve meets the radiator.</strong> Usually the valve tail. We reseal or replace it.</li>
        <li><strong>Green or white crust on the valve.</strong> A slow weep that has dried. Get it fixed before it gets worse.</li>
      </ul>
      <div class="brx-danger"><p><strong>Radiator valve leaking now?</strong> Put a bowl and towels under it. Close both valves on that radiator, turning them clockwise. If it will not stop, turn off the water at the stopcock and call <a href="tel:01515580334">{PHONE}</a>.</p></div>
      <p>Is the water coming from somewhere you cannot see, like under the floor? Our <a href="/leak-detection-liverpool">leak detection</a> service finds it. We lift boards and pressure test, and we can write a report for your insurer.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="brx-sec" id="how">
    <div class="brx-wrap">
      <h2>How do you replace a radiator valve?</h2>
      <p class="brx-answer">One valve takes 30 minutes to an hour. Six valves usually take about half a day.</p>
      <ol class="sg-steps">
        <li><strong>Check (5 minutes).</strong> We look at the valves, pipes and system. We confirm the price before we start.</li>
        <li><strong>Protect and isolate (10 minutes).</strong> Dust sheets down. We drain down or use a freezing kit on the pipe.</li>
        <li><strong>Swap the valve (15 to 30 minutes).</strong> Old valve off. New TRV and lockshield on, with new seals.</li>
        <li><strong>Refill and bleed (10 minutes).</strong> We refill, bleed the air out and reset the pressure.</li>
        <li><strong>Test (5 minutes).</strong> Heating on, every joint checked for leaks. We show you how to set the TRV.</li>
      </ol>
      <div class="brx-cta">
        <p><strong>Want new TRVs fitted this week?</strong> Call for a fixed price. £65 each, or up to six for £295.</p>
        <a href={TEL} class="brx-btn">Call {PHONE}</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="brx-sec brx-tint" id="smart">
    <div class="brx-wrap">
      <h2>Can you fit smart radiator valves?</h2>
      <p class="brx-answer">Yes. If you buy your own smart radiator valves, we fit them for £65 per radiator. We pair them and test them before we leave.</p>
      <p>We fit Tado, Hive, Drayton Wiser, Netatmo, Danfoss and most other brands. Most smart heads screw onto a standard TRV body. Some need an adaptor, which usually comes in the box.</p>
      <p>If your valve body is very old, worn or will not take the head, we fit a new TRV body first. We tell you the price before we start.</p>
      <p>Smart valves work best with a smart thermostat from the same brand. Our <a href="/smart-thermostat-installation-liverpool">smart thermostat installation</a> page covers that. Fitting your own smart thermostat is £120.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="brx-sec" id="which">
    <div class="brx-wrap">
      <h2>Which option is right for me?</h2>
      <p class="brx-answer">It depends on what is wrong and what you want. Here is our honest advice.</p>
      <div class="brx-table-wrap">
        <table class="brx-table stk stk-price lbl3">
          <thead><tr><th>Your situation</th><th>Price inc VAT</th><th>Best option</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td data-label="Your situation">One valve is stuck, broken or worn</td><td data-label="Price inc VAT">£65</td><td data-label="Best option">Single TRV</td></tr>
            <tr><td data-label="Your situation">Old manual valves all round the house</td><td data-label="Price inc VAT">£295</td><td data-label="Best option">Up to 6 TRVs</td></tr>
            <tr><td data-label="Your situation">You want simple on/off valves, like for like</td><td data-label="Price inc VAT">£65 per radiator</td><td data-label="Best option">Manual valve pair swap</td></tr>
            <tr><td data-label="Your situation">Water dripping from a valve</td><td data-label="Price inc VAT">First hour £120</td><td data-label="Best option">Leaking valve repair</td></tr>
            <tr><td data-label="Your situation">You want to control each room from your phone</td><td data-label="Price inc VAT">£65 per radiator</td><td data-label="Best option">Fit your own smart valves</td></tr>
            <tr><td data-label="Your situation">Radiators cold at the bottom</td><td data-label="Price inc VAT">Priced before we start</td><td data-label="Best option"><a href="/power-flushing-liverpool">Power flush</a>, not new valves</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>Need a new radiator too? See our <a href="/radiators-liverpool">radiators page</a>. A standard double panel radiator, supplied and fitted with a new TRV, is from £245.</p>
      <p>Got other small jobs? Our first hour is £120 on weekdays, and many small jobs fit in it. Make a list and we will price it before we start. See <a href="/small-plumbing-jobs-liverpool">small plumbing jobs</a>, such as <a href="/tap-replacement-liverpool">tap replacement</a> and <a href="/toilet-repair-liverpool">toilet repairs</a>.</p>
      <h3>Who does the work?</h3>
      <p>Our heating engineers are part of our team led by James. Work on the heating circuit is done by our Gas Safe registered engineers. We carry £2 million public liability insurance and give a 12-month workmanship guarantee.</p>
      <div class="brx-note"><p><strong>Fitting a dual-fuel towel rail?</strong> The electric element needs a safe electrical supply. Our partner firm <a href="https://www.idealelectricalsolutions.co.uk/">Ideal Electrical Solutions</a> can fit it.</p></div>
    </div>
  </section>
</ServiceGuide>
